// @run
// @log 11
// @log 110
// @log 11
// @log 12
// @log 13
// @log 2
// @log 1
// @log 11.5
// @log 110.5
// @log 1010
// @log 11
// @hint redundant type annotation: `inner` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `makeA` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `makeB` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `step` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `m1` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `m2` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `m3` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `pick` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `pick1` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `pick2` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `scale` is inferred as `f64`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `fA` is inferred as `f64`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `fB` is inferred as `f64`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `bump` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `shadower` is inferred as `i32`
// TWO FRAMES THAT EACH DECLARE THEIR OWN NESTED FUNCTION OF THE SAME NAME.
//
// `buildFnMap` keys every lifted function by NAME and keeps the FIRST occurrence, so the
// flat `fnIndexOf` handed EVERY frame the module's first function of that name. On master
// each `inner(10)` below therefore called `makeA`'s `inner` — and because the two bodies
// have the SAME SHAPE, that is not a crash but a SILENT WRONG ANSWER: `makeB()` printed
// `11` instead of `110`, with exit code 0. Where the colliding bodies did NOT agree in
// shape (a different arity, param type or return type) the same mis-resolution emitted an
// invalid module instead.
//
// `emitCall` now resolves the callee through `fnIndexOfInScopeChain` — the language's own
// rule, innermost declaring scope wins. The function-VALUE path was already scope-aware
// (`fnIndexOfInScope`), which is why a returned-closure spelling of this always worked and
// the plain CALL spelling did not.
//
// Every `@log` here is the value the lexical rule requires; on master, six of them differ.

// (1) the canonical collision — same shape, so master answers 11/11.
function makeA(): i32 {
  function inner(x: i32): i32 { return x + 1 }
  return inner(10)
}
function makeB(): i32 {
  function inner(x: i32): i32 { return x + 100 }
  return inner(10)
}
print(makeA())
print(makeB())

// (2) THREE frames sharing one name.
function m1(): i32 {
  function step(x: i32): i32 { return x + 1 }
  return step(10)
}
function m2(): i32 {
  function step(x: i32): i32 { return x + 2 }
  return step(10)
}
function m3(): i32 {
  function step(x: i32): i32 { return x + 3 }
  return step(10)
}
print(m1())
print(m2())
print(m3())

// (3) CALLED in the opposite order to declaration — first-wins is not an ordering
// artifact of the call sites.
function pick1(): i32 {
  function pick(): i32 { return 1 }
  return pick()
}
function pick2(): i32 {
  function pick(): i32 { return 2 }
  return pick()
}
print(pick2())
print(pick1())

// (4) an f64 payload: the wrong resolution is a wrong NUMBER, not a type error.
function fA(): f64 {
  function scale(x: f64): f64 { return x + 1.5 }
  return scale(10.0)
}
function fB(): f64 {
  function scale(x: f64): f64 { return x + 100.5 }
  return scale(10.0)
}
print(fA())
print(fB())

// (5) a nested function SHADOWS a top-level one of the same name. The top-level call
// must still reach the top-level function (the CONTROL that keeps the fix honest: a
// resolver that always preferred a nested declaration would break this line).
function bump(x: i32): i32 { return x + 1000 }
function shadower(): i32 {
  function bump(x: i32): i32 { return x + 1 }
  return bump(10)
}
print(bump(10))
print(shadower())
